WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.571
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:09.002
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.302 --> 00:00:16.099
Unit nine: Climate Justice. In this unit, you will discuss fairness in responding to climate change.

4
00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:18.421
climate justice.

5
00:00:19.121 --> 00:00:24.650
climate justice means the idea that climate action should be fair to the people most affected.

6
00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:28.169
Climate justice asks who should pay for climate damage.

7
00:00:28.869 --> 00:00:32.206
Your turn. Say: climate justice.

8
00:00:34.906 --> 00:00:36.255
climate justice.

9
00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.924
carbon inequality.

10
00:00:39.624 --> 00:00:45.153
carbon inequality means the unequal amount of carbon produced by rich and poor people.

11
00:00:45.453 --> 00:00:51.168
The richest ten percent cause about half of global emissions: that's carbon inequality.

12
00:00:51.868 --> 00:00:55.449
Your turn. Say: carbon inequality.

13
00:00:58.149 --> 00:00:59.557
carbon inequality.

14
00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:01.981
loss and damage.

15
00:01:02.681 --> 00:01:07.954
loss and damage means harm from climate change that cannot be avoided or adapted to.

16
00:01:08.254 --> 00:01:11.601
Small islands are demanding funds for loss and damage.

17
00:01:12.301 --> 00:01:16.125
Your turn. Say: loss and damage.

18
00:01:18.825 --> 00:01:20.546
loss and damage.

19
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:23.004
adaptation fund.

20
00:01:23.704 --> 00:01:29.593
adaptation fund means money to help communities adjust to climate impacts.

21
00:01:29.893 --> 00:01:32.960
The adaptation fund paid for stronger sea walls.

22
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.882
Your turn. Say: adaptation fund.

23
00:01:39.582 --> 00:01:40.931
adaptation fund.

24
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:43.545
vulnerable nation.

25
00:01:44.245 --> 00:01:48.891
vulnerable nation means a country at high risk from climate impacts.

26
00:01:49.191 --> 00:01:52.178
Cambodia is considered a climate-vulnerable nation.

27
00:01:52.878 --> 00:01:55.913
Your turn. Say: vulnerable nation.

28
00:01:58.613 --> 00:01:59.905
vulnerable nation.

29
00:02:00.917 --> 00:02:02.359
climate finance.

30
00:02:03.059 --> 00:02:08.472
climate finance means money from rich countries to support climate action in poorer ones.

31
00:02:08.772 --> 00:02:12.374
Climate finance promises have often not been met.

32
00:02:13.074 --> 00:02:16.504
Your turn. Say: climate finance.

33
00:02:19.204 --> 00:02:20.507
climate finance.

34
00:02:21.517 --> 00:02:22.738
polluter pays.

35
00:02:23.438 --> 00:02:27.609
polluter pays means the principle that those who cause pollution should pay for it.

36
00:02:27.909 --> 00:02:31.534
The polluter pays principle is widely accepted in law.

37
00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:35.629
Your turn. Say: polluter pays.

38
00:02:38.329 --> 00:02:39.505
polluter pays.

39
00:02:40.517 --> 00:02:41.750
just transition.

40
00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:47.073
just transition means moving to clean energy without leaving workers behind.

41
00:02:47.373 --> 00:02:50.929
A just transition protects coal miners' livelihoods.

42
00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:54.896
Your turn. Say: just transition.

43
00:02:57.596 --> 00:02:58.888
just transition.

44
00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:02.127
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:02.427 --> 00:03:06.632
Rithy, who should pay for climate damage in countries like Cambodia?

46
00:03:07.917 --> 00:03:11.518
It would seem fair that the biggest polluters contribute the most.

47
00:03:12.817 --> 00:03:18.267
That's the polluter pays principle. But richer nations argue that everyone emits now.

48
00:03:19.583 --> 00:03:24.035
Arguably, yes. However, their historical emissions are far larger.

49
00:03:25.350 --> 00:03:28.661
And carbon inequality exists within countries too.

50
00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:34.472
True. It could be argued that wealthy individuals should pay more as well.

51
00:03:35.783 --> 00:03:38.375
What about workers in polluting industries?

52
00:03:39.683 --> 00:03:45.052
That's where a just transition matters. Nobody should lose their livelihood without support.

53
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.756
Grammar focus: Hedging language.

54
00:03:49.056 --> 00:03:52.402
Hedging language makes your claims more careful and academic.

55
00:03:52.702 --> 00:03:56.745
It would seem, it could be argued that, arguably, and tend to.

56
00:03:57.045 --> 00:04:01.599
For example: Arguably, rich nations bear more responsibility.

57
00:04:01.899 --> 00:04:07.010
Hedging makes academic arguments sound balanced and careful rather than absolute.

58
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:11.354
Now read and listen. Who Pays for a Warming World?.

59
00:04:11.654 --> 00:04:15.929
Climate change affects everyone, but not equally.

60
00:04:17.217 --> 00:04:22.699
Countries that have contributed least to global emissions tend to suffer the most severe impacts.

61
00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:29.895
Rising seas, floods and droughts threaten farmers in vulnerable nations such as Cambodia.

62
00:04:31.183 --> 00:04:36.840
It could be argued that wealthy countries, which industrialised first, owe a moral debt.

63
00:04:38.150 --> 00:04:42.994
At recent climate summits, a fund for loss and damage was finally agreed.

64
00:04:44.283 --> 00:04:48.709
However, climate finance has arguably fallen short of what was promised.

65
00:04:50.017 --> 00:04:56.707
Many experts suggest that a just transition must also protect workers in fossil-fuel industries.

66
00:04:58.017 --> 00:05:03.522
Climate justice, it would seem, is as much about fairness as about carbon.

67
00:05:04.817 --> 00:05:07.225
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:07.525 --> 00:05:11.858
Moving to clean energy without leaving workers behind is a blank.

69
00:05:12.158 --> 00:05:18.343
A, just transition. B, loss and damage. C, carbon inequality.

70
00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.244
The answer is A: just transition.

71
00:05:26.750 --> 00:05:28.413
Which sentence is hedged?

72
00:05:28.713 --> 00:05:37.219
A, Rich nations must pay.. B, It could be argued that rich nations should pay.. C, Rich nations always pay..

73
00:05:41.217 --> 00:05:45.643
The answer is B: It could be argued that rich nations should pay..

74
00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:49.695
Poor communities blank suffer first.

75
00:05:49.995 --> 00:05:53.846
A, tend to. B, are tending. C, tends.

76
00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:00.047
The answer is A: tend to.

77
00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.560
The principle that polluters should pay is called blank.

78
00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:10.928
A, adaptation fund. B, polluter pays. C, climate finance.

79
00:06:14.917 --> 00:06:17.648
The answer is B: polluter pays.

80
00:06:19.150 --> 00:06:22.903
According to the text, what was finally agreed at recent summits?

81
00:06:23.203 --> 00:06:29.898
A, a ban on coal. B, a loss and damage fund. C, free energy.

82
00:06:33.883 --> 00:06:37.462
The answer is B: a loss and damage fund.

83
00:06:38.950 --> 00:06:44.865
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should rich countries pay for climate damage in poorer countries?

84
00:06:50.165 --> 00:06:56.576
Part two: Use hedging language: give your view, two reasons and one counter-argument.

85
00:07:02.876 --> 00:07:15.725
It would seem fair that they contribute more. Firstly, their historical emissions are much higher; secondly, they have greater resources. It could be argued, however, that fast-growing economies should also share the cost.

86
00:07:16.025 --> 00:07:18.419
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:19.417 --> 00:07:23.485
Great work today! You learned eight new words and hedging language.

88
00:07:23.785 --> 00:07:28.516
Today's value is caring for nature. Care for plants, birds and our planet.

89
00:07:28.816 --> 00:07:31.178
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

90
00:07:31.478 --> 00:07:34.270
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

